# Brand: aefi (lowercase)

The product brand is **aefi**, always lowercase. Never write `Aefi` or `AEFI` in product/brand contexts.

## Canonical forms

| Surface | Form |
| --- | --- |
| Brand / wordmark | `aefi` |
| Domain | `aefi.io` |
| Prose | `aefi helps autonomous agents...` |
| Product refs | `aefi API`, `aefi Provider Search`, `built by aefi` |

## Rules

- Prefer lowercase even at sentence start when referring to the brand (same pattern as npm, eBay, iOS)
- Do not expand the name as an acronym in user-facing copy
- Code identifiers may use `aefi` prefixes (`aefi-`, `AEFI_` only if an existing code convention requires SCREAMING_SNAKE — prefer `aefi_` / `Aefi` class names only when language style guides force PascalCase types; brand prose still stays `aefi`)
- For TypeScript/JavaScript **exported type/class names**, PascalCase `Aefi...` is acceptable as an identifier; comments and docs still say `aefi`

## Why

The logo wordmark starts with a lowercase **a** and flows into lowercase **fi**. Title case mismatches the mark, feels less software-native, and collides with the uppercase medical acronym AEFI.
